  First question. This is the fork that determines everything else, so here's the comparison before I ask:

  [1mThe fork:[0m how does the app know [3mwhich[0m environment it's in at runtime? Since there's no build step, there's nothing
  that can substitute a value at package time — the choice is between shipping all environments and picking one at
  runtime, versus shipping one and editing/replacing a file per deploy.

  - [1mAll environments in one file, picked at runtime[0m (e.g. from hostname or a [38;5;153m?env=[39m param): switching is zero-friction,
    one file holds [38;5;153mdev[39m/[38;5;153mstaging[39m/[38;5;153mprod[39m. Costs: your staging and prod URLs are visible in the shipped source, and you need a
    selection rule that's right in every context.
  - [1mOne file per environment, swapped at deploy[0m ([38;5;153msettings.js[39m is the live one, [38;5;153msettings.<env>.js[39m are the sources): each
    deploy ships only its own URLs. Costs: switching locally means copying a file, and it's easy to deploy a stale one.
  - [1mSingle file, edited by hand[0m — closest to today, just relocated. Cheapest now; doesn't actually deliver "easier to
    change environments," so I'd only take it if the real goal is just tidying.

  Cheap to change later: the selection rule, and adding environments. Expensive to change later: whether consumers read
  settings synchronously at load or [38;5;153mawait[39m them — that decision propagates into every call site, so I want to settle it
  with this answer.

  [1mMy recommendation: all environments in one file, selected by hostname.[0m For a static app with no build step it's the
  only option that makes switching genuinely frictionless, and API base URLs aren't secrets — they're discoverable from
  network traffic regardless.
